1. `lower bound` : array should be sorted for efficient usage and then creater a pointer `*ptr` and then 
    assign lower_bound this will take three parameters first from where to apply second till where to apply + 1
    th position and then third on which number we have to apply lower bound . and then dereference the pointer. 
    And then before printing pointer  , we made a contain that if pointer equal to a+n the last position + 1 th 
    index then no lowerbound for that number is possible . 
    
    		   int n;
    cin >> n;

    int a[5];
    for(int i = 0 ; i < n ;i++){
        cin >> a[i];
    }
    sort(a,a+n);
    int *ptr = lower_bound(a,a+n,5);
    if(ptr == (a+n)){
        cout << "NOT found!" << '\n';
        return ;
    }
    cout << *ptr << '\n';
    
    
2. `upper_bound` : Upper bound will work same as lower_bound but that will find exactly next greater than number possible. 


3. `time complexity` : lower bound as well as upper bound both works on `O(log(N))` that N is the number of elements in the array. 


4. `Using specific range` : we can even give specific range it is not necessary to give full range always `int *ptr = lower_bount(a+3,a+n,5);`
    this will work from 3rd index to n-1th index . 
    
    
5. `Upper bound and lower bound for vector array` : this will work exactly same to same as they did in static array , that 
    is core logic and backend working would be same just different syntax that is we will create a vector array instead of a 
    static , use iterator instead of pointer and then we check d.end().
    	   vector <int> d(n);
    for(int i = 0 ; i < n ;i++){
        cin >> d[i];
    }
    sort(d.begin(),d.end());
    auto it = upper_bound(d.begin(),d.end(),5);
    if(it == d.end()) {
        cout << "NOT! Found" << '\n';
        return ;
    }
    
    cout << *it << '\n';
    
    
6. `upper bound and lower bound in sets ` : they work exactly as they did in vector array but there will be minute difference
    in the synax , first create a set and then insert values and then directly create a iterator because our set is already
    sorted and then in iterator write `d.lower_bound(5) or d.upper_bound(5)` because if we write `lower_bound(d.begin(),d.end(),5`
    then this will work on `O(N)` wherease first one will work at `O(log(N))` and you won't encounter TLE. and after writing
    iterator just dereference it . 
    1. ```cpp
	   set <int> d;
    for(int i = 0 ; i < n ; i++){
        int a;
        cin >> a;
        d.insert(a);
    }
    auto it = d.lower_bound(5);
    if(it == d.end()){
        cout << "NOT! Found" << '\n';
        return ;
    }
    
    cout << *it << '\n';
	   ```
	   
	   
7. `Upper bound and lower bound in maps` : They also work similar to upper bound and lower bound in maps means in this 
    also we have to create a iterator that will be like `auto it = m.upper_bound(5);` because this will work for `O(log(N))` 
    and in maps upper bound and lowerbound works on the keys and we don't need to sort the maps because they are already 
    sorted and after dererencing the iterator we can use that iterator to print either key or corresponding values
        auto it = r.upper_bound(5);
    cout << (*it).second << '\n';
    
    
8. `Index Conversion for the iterator used to find either lower bound or upper bound`: To get the integer index, subtract the 
    beginning iterator `int idx = lower_bound(a.begin(), a.end(), x) - a.begin();` works for vectors and static_array.
    
